{"causes":["Notable Roman influence in Britain during the 5th century CE"],"effects":["The development of Christian liturgical practices in Wales, shaped by Saint David's pilgrimage and Non's motherhood"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"rome","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"non","targetName":"Non","context":"during his pilgrimage to Rome in the 7th century"},{"sourceSlug":"wales","sourceName":"Christianity in Wales","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"saint-david","targetName":"Saint David","context":"its liturgical practices and artistic traditions"},{"sourceSlug":"britain","sourceName":"Roman Britain","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"non","targetName":"Non","context":"around 500 CE"}],"places":[{"name":"Wales","role":"Birthplace and Cultural Hub"},{"name":"Rome","role":"Place of Pilgrimage"},{"name":"Roman Britain","role":"Region of Non's Birth"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Non","type":"Reference article","slug":"non"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2487289","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Non"],"tags":["Religious Figures","comparative-religion","doctrine-development","cultural-diffusion"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Non (also Nonna, Nonita; c. 5th century CE, died c. 600 CE) was a Welsh saint — venerated in both Wales and Brittany — who is traditionally identified as the mother of Saint David (Dewi Sant), the patron saint of Wales (c. 500–589 CE). Her feast day is observed on 3 March in the Catholic Church and in the Welsh and Breton traditions. She is one of the relatively rare female saints of the early Celtic Christian tradition who achieved widespread veneration — her cult centred in the southwest of Wales (Pembrokeshire), particularly at the chapel of St. Non's near St. David's, Pembrokeshire, which is associated with the site of David's birth.

According to hagiographic tradition, Non was a nun who was raped by a local chieftain (in some versions identified as Sant, king of Ceredigion) and conceived David — whose birth was accompanied by miraculous signs including a great storm and a radiant light. After David's birth, Non is said to have adopted a life of religious penance and holiness, eventually travelling to Brittany (northwest France) where she established a religious community and died. The chapel at St. Non's Bay, with its holy well (traditionally the well that sprang at David's birth), remains a pilgrimage site.

Non's story illustrates several characteristic features of early Celtic Christian hagiography: the association of sanctity with miraculous birth circumstances, the pattern of holy women in the early Welsh Christian tradition, the close connection between the Welsh and Breton churches (reflecting the historical migration of Celtic peoples from Britain to Armorica in the 5th–6th centuries), and the use of well-cult sites as foci of religious devotion in the Celtic Christian landscape.","subjects":["Celtic Christianity","Welsh History","Classical Era","Saints","Medieval Wales","Female Saints","Early Medieval History","Brittany"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Non's life and legacy offer a nuanced window into the complex web of cultural, religious, and familial relationships that defined early medieval Britain.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
